## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to apply k-way merge algorithms to partially sorted data, a common scenario in data processing pipelines and external sorting. It emphasizes understanding how the number of runs (r) impacts both time complexity and memory usage, connecting theoretical concepts with practical implementation.

## Key Concepts

- **k-way merge**: Efficient merging of multiple sorted sequences
- **Run detection**: Identifying sorted segments in a single linear pass
- **Min-heap**: Priority queue for selecting minimum elements efficiently
- **Complexity analysis**: O(n log r) time, O(r) auxiliary space
- **Edge cases**: Empty input, duplicate elements, and single-run scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor() { 
    this.heap = []; 
  }

  size() { 
    return this.heap.length; 
  }

  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this._heapifyUp(this.heap.length - 1);
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return null;
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const last = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = last;
      this._heapifyDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }

  _heapifyUp(index) { /* implementation */ }
  _heapifyDown(index) { /* implementation */ }
}

function kWayMerge(arr) {
  // Edge case: empty input
  if (arr.length === 0) return [];

  // Detect runs
  const runs = [];
  let start = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= arr.length; i++) {
    if (i === arr.length || arr[i-1] > arr[i]) {
      runs.push({start, end: i-1, index: start});
      start = i;
    }
  }

  // Edge case: r=1 (already sorted)
  if (runs.length === 1) return arr.slice();

  // Initialize heap with first elements
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  const result = [];

  runs.forEach((run, i) => {
    heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId: i});
  });

  // Merge process
  while (heap.size() > 0) {
    const {value, runId} = heap.pop();
    result.push(value);

    const run = runs[runId];
    if (run.index < run.end) {
      run.index++;
      heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId});
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply DFS for topological ordering and then DP-style relaxation on a DAG to compute shortest paths without a heap. Also checks path reconstruction via a parent array.

## Key Concepts

- DFS-based topological sort
- DAG single-source shortest path via relaxation in topo order
- Path reconstruction with a parent array
- Complexity: O(n+m)

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(n, edges, start, target) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) g[u].push([v, w]);
  const visited = Array(n).fill(false);
  const topo = [];
  function dfs(u){
    visited[u] = true;
    for (const [v] of g[u]) if (!visited[v]) dfs(v);
    topo.push(u);
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!visited[i]) dfs(i);
  topo.reverse();
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);
  dist[start] = 0;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u] === INF) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of g[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        parent[v] = u;
      }
    }
  }
  if (dist[target] === INF) return {time: -1, path: []};
  const path = [];
  for (let x = target; x != -1; x = parent[x]) path.push(x);
  path.reverse();
  return {time: dist[target], path};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle multiple sources or tie-breaking paths?
- How would you adapt if the graph could contain cycles (cycle detection and fallback to Dijkstra)?

## Purpose
This problem tests sliding-window aggregation with per-key state management, lazy expiration strategies, and top-K extraction using a heap. It integrates multiple data structures: hashtables for O(1) key lookups, per-key queues for temporal ordering, and a heap for maintaining ranked sums.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding window maintenance**: Events expire once they exceed the window width W
- **Per-key accumulation**: Each key maintains its own queue of (timestamp, value) pairs
- **Lazy eviction**: Stale entries are removed only when encountered, avoiding unnecessary cleanup
- **Amortized efficiency**: The cost of expiration is distributed across active operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedTopKMap {
  constructor(windowWidth) {
    this.W = windowWidth;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = new MinHeap();
  }

  addEvent(key, value, ts) {
    if (!this.map.has(key)) {
      this.map.set(key, { sum: 0, queue: [] });
    }
    const entry = this.map.get(key);
    entry.queue.push({ ts, value });
    entry.sum += value;
    this.heap.push({ sum: entry.sum, key });
  }

  advanceTime(now) {
    // Lazy cleanup handled in topKKeys
  }

  topKKeys(K, now) {
    const results = [];
    while (results.length < K && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      const entry = this.map.get(top.key);
      if (!entry) continue;
      this._expire(entry.queue, now);
      if (Math.abs(entry.sum - top.sum) > 0.0001) continue;
      results.push(top.key);
    }
    return results;
  }

  _expire(queue, now) {
    while (queue.length && queue[0].ts <= now - this.W) {
      entry.sum -= queue.shift().value;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle negative values, where sum could decrease over time?
- Could you support variable window sizes per key if needed?
- What memory bounds exist, and how could you proactively prune empty keys?

## Why This Question Matters

This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to extend standard grid DP problems with additional state dimensions. Modeling exact-turn constraints is essential in path-planning scenarios such as robot navigation, vehicle routing, and game movement where turn penalties or restrictions exist.

## Core Concepts

- **State Design**: Extending traditional grid DP with turn count and last direction dimensions
- **Transition Logic**: Increment turn count when direction changes between moves
- **Space Optimization**: Using row-wise or 2-layer rolling arrays to reduce memory from O(n*m*K) to O(m*K)
- **Edge Cases**: Handling obstacles, impossible turn requirements, and boundary conditions

## Reference Implementation

```javascript
function maxPathSumWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [INF, INF])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === -1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (j + 1 < m && grid[i][j+1] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (i > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0] = Math.max(
              dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0],
              dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i][j+1]
            );
          }
          dp[i][j+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][t][0], dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i][j+1]);
        }
        if (i + 1 < n && grid[i+1][j] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (j > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1] = Math.max(
              dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1],
              dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i+1][j]
            );
          }
          dp[i+1][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t][1], dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i+1][j]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1].flat());
  return result === INF ? -1 : result;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify the solution to allow movement in all four directions while avoiding cycles?
- How would you adapt the approach to handle obstacles with varying traversal costs or time penalties?
- What modifications would enable finding the minimum path sum instead of the maximum?

## Why This Is Asked

The problem mirrors real-world build systems and DAG validations, requiring both cycle detection and a valid execution order.

## Key Concepts

- DFS with 3-color marking to detect cycles
- Topological sort via postorder push
- Handling multiple components in a possibly disconnected graph

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfsTopo(graph) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const color = Array(n).fill(0); // 0 white, 1 gray, 2 black
  const order = [];
  const cycles = [];
  function dfs(u) {
    color[u] = 1;
    for (const v of graph[u] || []) {
      if (color[v] === 0) {
        if (dfs(v)) return true;
      } else if (color[v] === 1) {
        cycles.push([u, v]);
        return true;
      }
    }
    color[u] = 2;
    order.push(u);
    return false;
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (color[i] === 0) {
      if (dfs(i)) break;
    }
  }
  return { topo: order.reverse(), cycle: cycles.length ? cycles : null };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you return the exact cycle path rather than just edge pairs?
- What are the time and space complexities for dense vs sparse graphs?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess understanding of Trie data structure and practical prefix search used in autocompletion; tests familiarity with basic data structures and iteration over strings.

## Key Concepts
- Trie structure with 26-way children
- insert/search/startsWith time complexity O(m)
- memory proportional to number of unique prefixes

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next = new Array(26).fill(null); this.end=false; } }
class Trie { constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); }
 insert(word){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of word){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) p.next[idx]=new TrieNode(); p=p.next[idx]; } p.end=true; }
 search(word){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of word){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) return false; p=p.next[idx]; } return p.end; }
 startsWith(prefix){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) return false; p=p.next[idx]; } return true; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for memory?
- How would you extend to support deletion or word counts?

