- How would you reduce space to O(1) for the tabulation approach?
- How would you adapt for mixed negative values or mandatory picks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to frame a sign-assignment problem as a DP and implement both memoization and tabulation, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP state reduction to subset sum
- Space optimization with a 1D array
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tabulation and Memo versions
function countTargetSumTab(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const dp = Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const n of nums) {
    for (let t = target; t >= n; t--) dp[t] += dp[t-n];
  }
  return dp[target];
}

function countTargetSumMemo(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, sum){
    if (sum > target) return 0;
    if (i === nums.length) return sum === target ? 1 : 0;
    const key = i+':'+sum;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i+1, sum+nums[i]) + dfs(i+1, sum);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0,0);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of in-place merging, a practical skill when space is constrained.

## Key Concepts
- In-place merging with end pointers
- Time O(m+n), space O(1)
- Edge cases: empty arrays, duplicates, negative numbers

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergeInPlace(A, m, B, n) {
  let i = m - 1, j = n - 1, k = m + n - 1;
  while (j >= 0) {
    if (i >= 0 && A[i] > B[j]) {
      A[k--] = A[i--];
    } else {
      A[k--] = B[j--];
    }
  }
}
```

